1 OCCU Sorenskriver, Eidsvollsmann - sorenskriver i Folio, senere justitiarius i høiesteret 
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.falsen.se/
2 CONT
2 CONT http://data.eidsvollsmenn.no/tng/getperson.php?personID=I25931&tree=Eidsvollsmenn
2 CONT
2 CONT http://snl.no/.nbl_biografi/Christian_Magnus_Falsen/utdypning
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_Magnus_Falsen
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.vigerust.net/oslo/tillegg_falsen97.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Magnus Falsen (September 14, 1782 – January 13, 1830) was a Norwegian constitutional father, statesman, jurist, and historian. He was an important member of the constitutional assembly and was one of the writers of the c
2 CONC onstitutional laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Magnus Falsen was born at Christiania. He was the son of Enevold de Falsen (1755–1808), a dramatist and author of a famous war song "Til vaaben". In 1808 he became circuit judge at Follo, and after Denmark ceded Norway t
2 CONC o Sweden in 1814 he played an important part in politics. He upheld King Christian Frederick and, after the separation of Norway from Denmark, assisted in drafting a constitution for Norway, which was modeled upon that adopted by 
2 CONC France in 1791 and which was approved on 17 May 1814 by the Eidsvold. He was also strongly inspired by Thomas Jefferson and the Constitution of the United States of America. He is often called The Father of the Norwegian Constitut
2 CONC ion - "Grunnlovens far". In 1822 he was appointed Attorney General of the Kingdom, a post which he held for three years. In 1825 he became bailiff for Bergen, and in 1827 president of the Supreme Court. In 1828 he suffered from a 
2 CONC stroke and did not return to the office. Christian Magnus Falsen is buried at Gamlebyen Churchyard. Next to his gravestone is the gravestone of his second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1804 he married Anna Birgitte Munch (1787-1810), with whom he had the son Enevold Munch Falsen (1810–80). In 1811, after her death, he married Elisabeth Severine Böckmann (1782-1848). She was the widow of Brede Stoltenberg, a b
2 CONC rother of the tradesman Gregers Stoltenberg. With her he had the children Henrik Anton Falsen (1813–66) and Elisabeth Christine Falsen (1820–76).
2 CONT
2 CONT His principal work is Norges Historie (1823–24), a history of Norway to 1319 AD.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1825 to 1827 he was County Governor of Hordaland.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Publications
2 CONT
2 CONT Daa, (Biography of C. M. Falsen) (Christiana, 1860)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vullum, (Biography of C. M. Falsen) (Christiana, 1881)
